---
import BaseLayout from "../../components/BaseLayout.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

const project = projects[2];
---
<BaseLayout title="Active Directory Attack Lab" description={project.description} work={{ type: "CreativeWork" }}>
  <header class="page-hero container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Project / Lab engineering</p>
    <h1>Active Directory Attack Lab</h1>
    <a class="text-link" href="/projects/">← All projects</a>
    <p class="lead">An isolated Windows domain for studying how endpoint configuration and authentication affect domain-wide exposure.</p>
  </header>
  <section class="container section">
    <figure class="lab-topology">
      <div class="topology-node dc-node"><span>Domain controller</span><strong>Windows Server 2019</strong><small>Active Directory Domain Services</small></div>
      <div class="topology-branches">
        <div class="topology-node"><span>Domain member</span><strong>Windows 10 · Workstation A</strong></div>
        <div class="topology-node"><span>Domain member</span><strong>Windows 10 · Workstation B</strong></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>Logical topology of the isolated lab. The workstation labels are generic.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="content-layout">
      <aside class="side-note"><span>Research question</span><strong>How far can an endpoint weakness reach when systems share an identity boundary?</strong></aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Environment</h2>
        <p>I configured a Windows Server 2019 domain controller and joined two Windows 10 workstations by hand. This gave me a controlled environment for comparing workstation behavior, domain authentication, and identity permissions.</p>
        <h2>Security concepts</h2>
        <p>The exercises covered Windows credential storage, shortcut handling, Kerberos service accounts, legacy name resolution, and NTLM authentication. Several paths reached domain-level impact in the lab.</p>
        <h2>What I learned</h2>
        <p>Assessing an authentication issue requires tracing the affected identity, the systems that trust it, and the permissions it carries. Controls such as signing, credential isolation, and tighter service account management address different parts of that chain.</p>
        <div class="tag-list">{project.stack.map((tag) => <span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
